* Last dog watch (18:27)  The pilot, Mr Tregenza of Falmouth, came aboard from the cutter to pilot her in.
* Last dog watch (18:27)  The pilot says: Keep the fair way and the lead going; there is a narrow deep channel of sixteen or eighteen fathoms all the way into Carrick Road. The Black Rock lies nearly in the middle of the entrance and shows itself at half tide; the eastern channel is the better, and you may borrow on St Mawes side in five or six fathoms. Killiganoon house on with Mylor Point, north a quarter east, carries you in mid-channel through Carrick Road; St Keverne church open east of Pendennis Point leads into St Just Pool. Moored in the Road, keep the hawse open to the southward, for southerly winds throw in much sea. Carrick Road, between St Mawes and Falmouth banks, seven to seventeen fathoms, the usual anchorage for ships of war; a small vessel may lie off the town in three fathoms, the packets' quay under Pendennis. High water at Falmouth about a quarter past four in the morning, the tide rising some 16 feet; the flood will serve from about a quarter past ten at night.
  Last dog watch (18:27)  The pilot's news: Britain at war with the Batavian Republic, France and Spain.
  Last dog watch (18:28)  The Gray bearing NW, distant four leagues.
* Last dog watch (18:28)  The pilot has her by his marks: 50° 05' N, 5° 00' W; the account is brought to it, and he keeps it there while his marks are in sight.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By the master's reckoning she is in the approaches to Falmouth: allowing the ebb, half a knot to the W by S.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'the leg': shaping a course for falmouth outer road.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Shaped a course for Falmouth outer road: NNW by account, 3 miles; the line passes the Old Wall within four cables. Allowing the ebb, a quarter of a knot to the W by S, steer NNW to make it good; the allowance holds till the tide turns, about half past eleven at night. Helm ordered: steer NNW (339°).
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'tend the sheets': trimming the sheets.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Trimming the sheets of the mizzen spanker, the fore topmast staysail and the jib.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'bearings': taking a bearing of the land.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Manacle Point bore SW, two miles by estimation: the account kept.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'bearings': taking a fix.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Fixed by cross bearings: Manacle Point SW, Nare Point W, Black Head SW; the lines met within a cable. The account moved a cable to the S: 50° 05' N, 5° 00' W by the fix, good to two cables along the shore and a cable off it.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'the lead in pilot water': heaving the lead.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Man the spanker sheet.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Man the fore topmast staysail sheet.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Man the jib sheet.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  A hand into the chains with the lead.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Leeway 2° to starboard.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Steady on NNW (339°).
* Last dog watch (18:29)  By standing order 'trim to the course': trimming sails.
  Last dog watch (18:29)  Braced twelve yards to the wind, 67° on the larboard bow; trimming the sheets of the mizzen spanker; the sheets of the fore topmast staysail and the jib stand as trimmed.
  Last dog watch, 1 bell (18:30)  1 bell.
  Last dog watch (18:30)  Trimmed the sheets of the fore topmast staysail, the jib and the spanker; 39° off the centreline.
  Last dog watch (18:30)  Man the spanker sheet.
  Last dog watch (18:30)  No bottom at twenty fathoms.
* Last dog watch (18:30)  Braced twelve yards to the wind; 60° to 63° from square.
* Last dog watch (18:31)  St Anthony's Head bearing N, steady and closing: distant three miles.
  Last dog watch (18:31)  Trimmed the spanker sheet; 36° off the centreline.
  Last dog watch (18:34)  By standing order 'the lead in pilot water': heaving the lead.
  Last dog watch (18:34)  A hand into the chains with the lead.
* Last dog watch (18:35)  And a half seventeen; good ground. One fathom of tide allowed by the epitome: sixteen fathoms and a half on the chart. The account kept.
  Last dog watch (18:35)  Standing order 'the anchorage' at a sounding: not carried out; the distance to carrick road is 4 miles by account, not under 1.8 miles.
  Last dog watch (18:39)  By standing order 'the lead in pilot water': heaving the lead.
  Last dog watch (18:39)  A hand into the chains with the lead.
* Last dog watch (18:40)  By the captain, on passage: coming to an anchor (the tide does not serve to enter Falmouth).
  Last dog watch (18:40)  The book 'on passage' unloaded: the leg, trim to the course, trim on a shift, tend the sheets, bearings, the morning sight, shorten sail for weather, the pilot boards, the pilot put off, the pilot off, under way again, a sail on passage, the lead in pilot water, the anchorage.
  Last dog watch (18:40)  The book 'at anchor' loaded: the tide to go in, under way for the port.
* Last dog watch (18:40)  Captain Bowen: at anchor (bound for Falmouth); his book 'at anchor' loaded: the tide to go in, under way for the port.
* Last dog watch (18:40)  All hands! (to come to anchor)
  Last dog watch (18:40)  Haul taut! In studding sails, royals and topgallants; up courses.
  Last dog watch (18:40)  All hands, bring ship to anchor! Stand by to take in the light sails.
* Last dog watch (18:40)  Quarter less fifteen; good ground. One fathom of tide allowed by the epitome: thirteen fathoms and a half on the chart. The account kept.
  Last dog watch (18:42)  All hands on deck.
  Last dog watch (18:43)  Let go the topsail sheets; clew up; haul down the jib. Helm down for WSW.
  Last dog watch (18:44)  Leeway 3° to starboard.
  Last dog watch (18:45)  Leeway 13° to starboard.
  Last dog watch (18:46)  Leeway 11° to starboard.
